\description{
Fits the area-level model of Fay and Herriot (1979) with the fixed part
replaced or corrected by a random forest (Bukhari et al., 2026), and predicts
the area parameters with their mean squared error (MSE) estimated by a
parametric bootstrap.
}
\details{
\strong{Model.} For area \eqn{d = 1, \dots, D}, \eqn{y_d = \theta_d + e_d} with
\eqn{e_d \sim N(0, \psi_d)}, \eqn{\psi_d} known, and
\eqn{\theta_d = f(x_d) + v_d}, \eqn{v_d \sim N(0, \sigma^2_v)}. With
\code{variant = "replace"}, \eqn{f} is a random forest; with \code{variant = "correct"}, \eqn{f(x_d) = x_d^\prime\beta + g(x_d)} with \eqn{g} a random
forest.

\strong{Algorithm \code{"fhrf"}} (Bukhari et al., 2026, Sections 2.3-2.4):
\enumerate{
\item Fit the linear Fay-Herriot model, giving \eqn{\hat\beta}, \eqn{\hat v_d} and
\eqn{\hat\sigma^2_v}.
\item Fit the forest to \eqn{y_d - \hat v_d} (\code{"replace"}, eq. 12) or to
\eqn{y_d - x_d^\prime\hat\beta - \hat v_d} (\code{"correct"}, eq. 8) over the
sampled areas, and take its out-of-bag predictions \eqn{\hat f(x_d)}
(eqs. 9, 13). Unsampled areas get the ordinary forest prediction.
\item \eqn{\hat\theta_d = \hat m_d + \gamma_d(y_d - \hat m_d)} with
\eqn{\hat m_d = \hat f(x_d)} or \eqn{x_d^\prime\hat\beta + \hat f(x_d)}
(column \code{synthetic}) and \eqn{\gamma_d = \hat\sigma^2_v/(\hat\sigma^2_v + \psi_d)}
(eqs. 10, 14). Unsampled areas get \eqn{\hat m_d}.
}

Bukhari et al. (2026) estimate \eqn{\sigma^2_v} with an EM algorithm. Its
fixed point is the maximum likelihood estimate, so \code{method = "ML"} gives the
same fit (checked numerically against \code{sae::eblupFH()}); the default is
REML, as in \code{\link[=pspline_sae]{pspline_sae()}}. The Fay-Herriot fits maximize the profile
likelihood of \eqn{\sigma^2_v} directly (generalized least squares for
\eqn{\beta}); they agree with \code{sae::eblupFH()}.

\strong{Algorithm \code{"merf"}.} Starting from step 1, repeat: fit the forest to the
target of step 2 with the current \eqn{\hat v_d} (and \eqn{\hat\beta}) and take
its out-of-bag predictions; refit the Fay-Herriot model with the forest
predictions as an offset (and the linear terms for \code{"correct"}, no fixed
effects for \code{"replace"}), giving new \eqn{\hat\sigma^2_v}, \eqn{\hat v_d} (and
\eqn{\hat\beta}). Stop when the change of \eqn{\hat\sigma^2_v}, divided by
\eqn{\hat\sigma^2_v + \bar\psi}, is below \code{tol}. A forest is piecewise
constant, so the area estimates keep moving slightly from one iteration to
the next and are not used in the criterion. This follows the MERF algorithm of Hajjem et
al. (2014) and Krennmair and Schmid (2022), written for unit-level data,
with the Fay-Herriot fit in place of the unit-level mixed model. It is not
a published area-level method; it is offered because in \code{"fhrf"}
\eqn{\hat\sigma^2_v} also absorbs the non-linearity that the linear model
misses. All forests of one fit share one seed. This option is experimental.

Out-of-bag predictions keep the forest from fitting the sampling errors of
the direct estimates. Every sampled area must be out of bag in at least one
tree, which needs enough trees.

\strong{Self-benchmarking.} With \code{bench_weight}, the final EBLUP step uses the
fixed part \eqn{\hat m_d + z_d^\prime\hat\alpha}, with \eqn{z_d = w_d\psi_d} (one
column per group of \code{bench_group}, zero for unsampled areas) and
\eqn{\hat\alpha} the generalized least squares coefficient of
\eqn{y_d - \hat m_d} on \eqn{z_d} with variances \eqn{\hat\sigma^2_v + \psi_d}. As in
the augmented model of Wang, Fuller and Qu (2008, eq. 23), this gives
\eqn{\sum_d w_d(y_d - \hat\theta_d) = 0} over the sampled areas of each group.
Applying the augmentation to the step with the forest as an offset is our
extension; it is not in Bukhari et al. (2026).

\strong{MSE.} Naive parametric bootstrap (Bukhari et al., 2026, eqs. 15-17):
\eqn{\theta^*_d = \hat m_d + v^*_d}, \eqn{y^*_d = \theta^*_d + e^*_d}, the whole
algorithm, forest included, is refitted on \eqn{y^*} and
\eqn{\mathrm{mse}_d = B^{-1}\sum_b(\hat\theta^*_d - \theta^*_d)^2}. A replicate whose
refit fails is replaced, up to \code{5 * B} attempts.

\strong{Random numbers.} The forests use seeds drawn from the R random number
stream; with \code{seed} given the whole fit is reproducible and the stream of the
session is restored afterwards.
